# wim codecpack (WP637): Windows Imaging Format -> one VERBATIM member per
# resource.
#
# Decomposes `sources/install.wim` (the bulk of every Windows install ISO)
# and wimlib's solid `.esd` into one member per RESOURCE, holding the bytes
# exactly as they lie in the file -- compressed resource bodies included,
# never decoded. The recipe (strip) is the WIR1 blob: WIM size + the member
# (idx,off,len) table + every byte OUTSIDE the member extents, verbatim (the
# 208-byte header, the offset/resource table, the XML data when it is not
# itself a resource, every gap with its junk, trailing junk). Bit-exactness
# is by construction: nothing is ever re-encoded, so no MS/wimlib encoder is
# needed or trusted. The map command makes the pack SEEKABLE (WP16b MRMP;
# one MEMBER range per resource extent, RECIPE ranges for the rest).
#
# WHY THIS IS A CONTAINERPACK AND NOT A CODEC. install.wim is entropy-coded
# (LZX/XPRESS per resource, LZMS in a solid .esd) and Microsoft's encoder is
# closed while wimlib's stream differs ("usually outperforms and outcompresses
# their closed-source Microsoft equivalents", wimlib.net/compression.html), so
# a bit-exact rebuild by re-encoding is unachievable. Keeping each resource as
# its own content-addressed member is what buys POSITION INDEPENDENCE: a size
# change in one file shifts every later resource's offset in the WIM, which is
# why segment-aligned dedupe finds ~nothing across two Windows versions; a
# member at its own address is the same bytes at the same address wherever the
# encoder put it.
#
# Solid/LZMS: resources that ALIAS one solid block have overlapping extents,
# and the helper unions overlapping extents into ONE member, so a solid WIM
# gets one verbatim member per solid chunk -- no decomposition inside solid
# compression, still bit-exact, still dedupable at chunk granularity.
#
# The helper is a single C11 binary (wim.c), libc only, streaming in <= 8 MiB
# windows (a WIM resource can be gigabytes; no command ever holds one in RAM).
# argv0 is the pack-dir-relative "bin/wim": the FS resolves a relative argv0
# containing '/' against the pack dir both at probe and at exec time (a BARE
# "wim" probes OK via <pack>/bin/ but the exec layer resolves $INVFS_TOOLS ->
# /usr/lib/invfs/tools -> PATH instead, so the pack would never fire).
# See wim.c for the full format doc and the refuse list.
# algo 28: builtins own 0..15, WINDOW_SRC 24, NBLI 25, packs 4/13/16-23,
# tests 40/41 (src/core/invarifs.h) -- 26 is the next free id.
name = wim
type = container
algo = 28
codec_id = 28
pack_version = 1
version = 1
min_read = 1
generation = 1
family = container
category = primary
provides = container
replaces =
conflicts =
priority = 100
caps = container|external
dec_mem = 0
# "MSWIM\0\0\0" at offset 0. The helper's strict header parse is the real
# gate: a magic-only match declines with exit 3.
sniff.offset = 0
sniff.magic = 4D5357494D000000
sniff.ext = wim,esd
enumerate = bin/wim enumerate {in} {out}
extract = bin/wim extract {in} {idx} {out}
# WP140: the whole member set in ONE header parse. install.wim carries ~10^5
# resources; the per-member path re-reads and re-parses the offset table on
# every call, which is O(n^2). This command parses once and writes
# "<dir>/<idx>" for every member the enumerate table names -- the same
# stream_copy over the same (off,len) extents, so the bytes are bit-identical
# to the per-member path by construction. Optional: a pack without this key is
# still extracted one member per exec, as before.
batch = bin/wim extract_batch {in} {dir} {out}
strip = bin/wim strip {in} {out}
rebuild = bin/wim rebuild {recipe} {dir} {out}
map = bin/wim map {in} {out}
# Opt into the FS-owned decomposition generation. This pack renders a v1
# (MRMP) map today, so the stamp is a no-op -- but a pack that emits v2
# without this key gets a map header whose generation is whatever the pack
# happened to write, and a later generation bump re-decomposes nothing.
decomp_gen = 1
# estimate = sum(member bytes) + 64 MiB (header-derived, never a trial run)
estimate = bin/wim estimate {in}
